<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lands of the Bohemian Crown</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1348–1918</span> · Also known as Crown of Bohemia, Bohemian Crown</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lands of the Bohemian Crown were the territories united under the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>: the Kingdom of Bohemia, the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, Silesia and, for long periods, Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a>. Charles IV defined them as a single union under the Crown of Bohemia in 1348. Ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> from 1526, they later passed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, and Bohemia, Moravia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-silesia">Czech Silesia</a> form today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown.jpg" alt="Lands of the Bohemian Crown" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Urmas assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bohemia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
